# CryptoPro Trader — environment configuration
# Copy to ".env" (or set these in your shell/host) as needed.
# Authentication is application-only (username + password), same method as
# CryptoPro Charts and CryptoPro Suite — no third-party SSO credentials
# required.

# Port the server listens on (default 3000).
# PORT=3000

# Set to "production" to mark session cookies Secure (HTTPS only).
# NODE_ENV=production

# --- Supabase (Postgres) — accounts & sessions persistence (single sign-on) ---
# To make this project's login reuse the SAME accounts as CryptoPro Charts /
# CryptoPro Suite (one shared Supabase database across the suite, Suite
# workflow rule 18), this must resolve to the exact same Supabase Postgres
# project Charts uses. As of 2026-07-24 Vercel's Supabase integration issues
# this project its own prefixed vars (below) that point at that same shared
# project — Charts gets CRYPTOPROCHARTS_*, Trader gets CRYPTOPROTRADER_*,
# same underlying database, separate var names. Tables are created with
# "create table if not exists", so this is a no-op on the schema either way.
# CRYPTOPROTRADER_POSTGRES_URL="postgres://...:6543/postgres?sslmode=require&pgbouncer=true"
# CRYPTOPROTRADER_POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING="postgres://...:5432/postgres?sslmode=require"
# (Older DBCRYPTOCHARTS_POSTGRES_URL[_NON_POOLING] / this project's own
# pre-existing trading_POSTGRES_URL* vars are still accepted as fallbacks —
# see src/db.js's CONN_VARS — for instant rollback if the integration above
# is ever reconfigured.)
# (generic fallbacks also accepted: POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING, POSTGRES_URL, DATABASE_URL)

# Without a connection string the database is disabled and sign-in/register
# return 503 — the rest of the dashboard still works.

# --- Cron cutover (Suite roadmap, "For Trader only") ---
# Vercel Cron (see vercel.json — this project is on Vercel Pro, so it runs
# GET /api/cron/dispatch natively every hour, no GitHub Actions involved)
# plus the individual GET /api/cron/evaluate|watchdog|daily-summary routes
# for direct/manual calls, all with an `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`
# header when this is set — set it on the Vercel project (Vercel auto-injects
# it into CRON_SECRET when you use the "Vercel Cron Jobs" integration, or set
# your own random value and it's sent verbatim). These GET routes accept
# ONLY this bearer token, never a signed-in session (a session-auth fallback
# on GET would be exploitable via SameSite=Lax cookies on a cross-site
# navigation) — without this set, scheduled runs 401 and only the
# dashboard's manual "Run now" (POST, TRADER_OWNER_UID below) works.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Accounts are shared Suite-wide (any CryptoPro Charts/Training/Suite account
# can sign in here too, registration is open) — but the cron routes' manual
# "Run now" trigger and per-job enable/disable/schedule-hour config control
# ONE shared trading engine, not caller-owned data, so they're restricted to
# a single owner account instead of "any signed-in account". Set this to
# your account id (the lowercase username you registered with). Unset = all
# of those dashboard routes are disabled for everyone (fail closed) —
# scheduled runs via CRON_SECRET are unaffected either way.
# TRADER_OWNER_UID=

# Gates real order placement from the cron routes, same shape as the CLI's
# `--execute` flag. MUST stay unset/false until the three-gate cutover
# checkpoint (memory/claude_md_archive.md "Node.js port": frozen-fixture
# parity, >=24h live shadow-run parity, state round-trip) has actually been
# run — this is not a "flip when ready" toggle to set casually, the live
# trading engine is still 100% Python and this must not race it into placing
# duplicate orders.
# CRON_EXECUTE=false

# --- Multi-tenant Phase 2: per-user Alpaca credentials ---
# 32 random bytes, base64. Encrypts each user's Alpaca key/secret at rest
# (AES-256-GCM, src/secretsCrypto.js) in the trader_alpaca_credentials table,
# so a database dump alone yields no usable API keys. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32          (or: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))")
# Unset => the /api/alpaca-credentials routes fail closed with 503 and no
# credential is ever stored in plaintext; everything else keeps working on the
# shared APCA_* env-var account below. Treat this like a database password:
# set it in the Vercel dashboard, never commit it. There is no key-rotation
# path yet — changing it makes every already-stored credential unreadable
# (users would have to reconnect their keys).
#
# IMPORTANT — use a DIFFERENT key per Vercel environment. Vercel's "add
# variable" form pre-ticks Production+Preview+Development, so it is easy to
# end up with one shared key; with the same key and the same Supabase
# database, any preview build of any branch (a publicly reachable URL) could
# decrypt every stored production credential. Add the variable three times,
# each scoped to exactly one environment, with a different generated value.
#
# Because all environments share ONE database, isolation is per-ROW, not per
# environment: trader_alpaca_credentials is keyed (uid, mode) and the write is
# an upsert, so connecting credentials for the same account from two
# environments replaces the ciphertext and the other environment can no longer
# read it. Rule: outside Production, never sign in as your real account — use
# a throwaway test account. (`vercel dev` runs against the shared database
# too.) The key_fp column makes a violation report itself as KeyMismatch
# rather than a silent "credential disconnected"; it diagnoses the problem, it
# does not prevent it.
# TRADER_CREDENTIALS_ENC_KEY=
